WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:03.467
-Our first guest tonight is
a very funny actor you know

00:00:03.467 --> 00:00:06.100
from movies like "This Is 40,"
"Anchorman," and "Clueless."

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:08.501
He returns to
the Marvel Cinematic Universe

00:00:08.501 --> 00:00:10.734
in "Ant-Man and the Wasp:
Quantumania,"

00:00:10.734 --> 00:00:13.367
which premieres in theaters
and IMAX February 17th.

00:00:13.367 --> 00:00:14.534
Please welcome back to the show

00:00:14.534 --> 00:00:16.868
our very good friend Paul Rudd,
everybody.

00:00:16.868 --> 00:00:19.300
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:27.167
♪♪

00:00:27.167 --> 00:00:35.033
♪♪

00:00:35.033 --> 00:00:36.734
Welcome back, my friend.
-Thanks, Seth.

00:00:36.734 --> 00:00:38.634
Thank you, Seth.
-I'm so happy to see you.

00:00:38.634 --> 00:00:40.367
-It's great to see you.

00:00:40.367 --> 00:00:42.634
-You are riding high
for a lot of reasons.

00:00:42.634 --> 00:00:44.100
One, movie coming out.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:46.167
More importantly,
for a guy like me,

00:00:46.167 --> 00:00:48.267
your Kansas City Chiefs,
Super Bowl champions.

00:00:48.267 --> 00:00:49.868
Congratulations.
-Yeah. Thank you very much.

00:00:49.868 --> 00:00:51.701
-You got to be at the game.
-I went.

00:00:51.701 --> 00:00:53.234
-Fantastic.
-Oh, my gosh.

00:00:53.234 --> 00:00:56.501
What a -- What a whirlwind
that experience is.

00:00:56.501 --> 00:00:59.267
And it's just thrilling,
you know, when your team wins.

00:00:59.267 --> 00:01:01.334
Especially I've been a fan
for a long, long time.

00:01:01.334 --> 00:01:03.467
So this is kind of
a new experience.

00:01:03.467 --> 00:01:05.100
-And you had your son, Jack,
with you.

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:07.100
And that must make it
extra special,

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:08.934
because, again, this is
something that you --

00:01:08.934 --> 00:01:10.601
he would not be a football fan
if it wasn't --

00:01:10.601 --> 00:01:12.534
or he wouldn't be a Chiefs fan
if it wasn't for you.

00:01:12.534 --> 00:01:14.767
-Right, 'cause I guess he
started kind of rooting for them

00:01:14.767 --> 00:01:16.767
when he was about 6 or so.
-Yeah.

00:01:16.767 --> 00:01:19.901
-He's 18 now, and it's true.

00:01:19.901 --> 00:01:21.567
Like, the best thing
is to be able

00:01:21.567 --> 00:01:23.701
to kind of share this
with your kid.

00:01:23.701 --> 00:01:27.534
So, yeah, it was just --
it was awesome.

00:01:27.534 --> 00:01:29.968
-So they won in 2020
when they won.

00:01:29.968 --> 00:01:31.834
That was, you know,
a big deal for,

00:01:31.834 --> 00:01:34.000
I mean, again,
you as well as Jack,

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:35.801
but it must have been
such a cool moment to share

00:01:35.801 --> 00:01:38.033
as a father and a son.
-It was.

00:01:38.033 --> 00:01:42.968
That whole run
really was just massive,

00:01:42.968 --> 00:01:44.434
and it started this whole thing.

00:01:44.434 --> 00:01:48.467
And it was such a nice feeling,
because, you know, in that year,

00:01:48.467 --> 00:01:50.133
in the first playoff game,

00:01:50.133 --> 00:01:52.467
the Chiefs were down by
24 points against the Texans.

00:01:52.467 --> 00:01:55.300
And it really was the first time
I did kind of have this,

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:57.400
"Why do I do this?"

00:01:57.400 --> 00:01:59.167
My son was so despondent.

00:01:59.167 --> 00:02:01.634
He was so sad,
just kind of walking around.

00:02:01.634 --> 00:02:03.667
Went into another room.
-Yeah.

00:02:03.667 --> 00:02:05.801
-And I really had kind of that
by myself.

00:02:05.801 --> 00:02:08.000
I was really upset and mad.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:10.701
'Cause I thought, this is not --
this is not worth it.

00:02:10.701 --> 00:02:12.267
It's just not worth
putting yourself

00:02:12.267 --> 00:02:13.767
through this kind of despair.

00:02:13.767 --> 00:02:16.868
I feel like -- I know all Eagles
fans are feeling this right now.

00:02:16.868 --> 00:02:19.367
I know all fans of other teams
are feeling this right now.

00:02:19.367 --> 00:02:21.267
-But I feel like -- So this is
a thing that happened to me

00:02:21.267 --> 00:02:22.868
that you did to Jack, right,

00:02:22.868 --> 00:02:24.968
which is Jack's also
never lived in Kansas City.

00:02:24.968 --> 00:02:27.701
-No. Never in his life,
and he's a die-hard Chiefs fan.

00:02:27.701 --> 00:02:28.767
-And I'm the same way.

00:02:28.767 --> 00:02:30.100
Like, I'm a huge
Pittsburgh Steelers fan

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:32.467
because of my dad.
He fully just did it to me.

00:02:32.467 --> 00:02:34.000
I remember the same thing
in my teens

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:35.667
when things were going bad
for the Steelers.

00:02:35.667 --> 00:02:37.467
I'd be so upset,
and my dad would say,

00:02:37.467 --> 00:02:38.534
"Look, it's not that important."

00:02:38.534 --> 00:02:40.400
I'm like,
"You taught me to care!"

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:42.734
-It's the truth. It's like
why do we do this to them?

00:02:42.734 --> 00:02:44.567
-I wonder, how does your wife
feel about it?

00:02:44.567 --> 00:02:46.734
Because that's the other thing,
is my wife,

00:02:46.734 --> 00:02:48.367
who's watched me go through
the roller coaster,

00:02:48.367 --> 00:02:50.367
has basically said, like,
"I don't know if I'm cool

00:02:50.367 --> 00:02:52.968
with like two more of these
in the house."

00:02:52.968 --> 00:02:54.234
-It might be three.

00:02:54.234 --> 00:02:56.234
My daughter is real young,
and she watched the game,

00:02:56.234 --> 00:02:58.868
and she wrote "Chiefs Kingdom"
in pen on her arms.

00:02:58.868 --> 00:03:00.133
And she was -- she loves --

00:03:00.133 --> 00:03:02.467
She goes, "I want
a Kadarius Toney jersey."

00:03:02.467 --> 00:03:05.734
She's young, but she is also
really into it.

00:03:05.734 --> 00:03:08.100
I know, why we brought this
into the house.

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:10.734
It's like adopting a dog and --

00:03:10.734 --> 00:03:12.100
-Like a super-old dog.

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:13.267
-A super-old dog.

00:03:13.267 --> 00:03:14.767
This is --
You're just bringing --

00:03:14.767 --> 00:03:17.434
We're inviting in
disappointment.

00:03:17.434 --> 00:03:18.467
[ Laughter ]

00:03:18.467 --> 00:03:19.667
-"We want a puppy."

00:03:19.667 --> 00:03:21.934
"Well, I got good news
and mostly bad news."

00:03:21.934 --> 00:03:23.934
-"This is not --

00:03:23.934 --> 00:03:25.234
The time that we're
going to have with him

00:03:25.234 --> 00:03:26.934
is going to be very special."
-Yeah.

00:03:26.934 --> 00:03:28.567
-"But it's going to be..."

00:03:28.567 --> 00:03:31.934
-Very, very short.
-"...short," yeah.

00:03:31.934 --> 00:03:33.734
-Alright, so I want
to ask about something.

00:03:33.734 --> 00:03:35.067
Right around Christmas,

00:03:35.067 --> 00:03:36.868
we do this thing called
"Day Drinking" on the show.

00:03:36.868 --> 00:03:40.033
And I was doing it with Lizzo,
and your name came up.

00:03:40.033 --> 00:03:41.334
-I saw it.
-Yeah.

00:03:41.334 --> 00:03:43.467
And again, it must have been
very exciting,

00:03:43.467 --> 00:03:44.501
because Lizzo, let me tell you,

00:03:44.501 --> 00:03:45.801
one of the most charming
human beings.

00:03:45.801 --> 00:03:46.868
I don't know if you've been
lucky enough to meet her.

00:03:46.868 --> 00:03:48.167
-I've never met her.
I watched --

00:03:48.167 --> 00:03:49.467
I mean, I watched.
It was on the show.

00:03:49.467 --> 00:03:52.200
And when I heard Lizzo
say my name, I was like...

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:54.300
I got all excited.

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:55.868
By the way, it's a weird thing

00:03:55.868 --> 00:03:58.767
when you're not expecting
to hear that and you hear it.

00:03:58.767 --> 00:04:01.434
And that segment was hilarious,
by the way.

00:04:01.434 --> 00:04:03.501
That whole -- Every time
you do it, it's so funny.

00:04:03.501 --> 00:04:05.567
-She really did
sort of raise the bar.

00:04:05.567 --> 00:04:08.634
I keep telling people
she's so deeply funny.

00:04:08.634 --> 00:04:10.367
Even while, like, inebriated,

00:04:10.367 --> 00:04:12.767
she, like, talks like
a comedy writer.

00:04:12.767 --> 00:04:15.501
-So funny, so charming.

00:04:15.501 --> 00:04:17.467
Yeah, it was great.

00:04:17.467 --> 00:04:20.367
-She also -- I've told this a
few times, but the great thing,

00:04:20.367 --> 00:04:22.267
to bring it back to my dad,
you know,

00:04:22.267 --> 00:04:24.300
one of her catchphrases
is "Bye, bitch."

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:26.334
-Right.
-And we left a message --

00:04:26.334 --> 00:04:28.667
we left a voicemail for my dad,
and she said --

00:04:28.667 --> 00:04:30.000
she ended it by saying,
"Bye, bitch."

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.267
-Yeah.

00:04:31.267 --> 00:04:34.501
-Then independent of me,
she got my dad's information

00:04:34.501 --> 00:04:37.200
and sent him flowers
with a note that said,

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:39.300
"I'm sorry
I called you a bitch."

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:40.601
[ Laughter ]

00:04:40.601 --> 00:04:42.968
And my brother and I
call him a bitch all the time.

00:04:42.968 --> 00:04:44.901
We've never apologized.

00:04:44.901 --> 00:04:46.167
-You owe him a lot of flowers.

00:04:46.167 --> 00:04:47.767
-Yeah, we owe him
a lot of flowers.

00:04:47.767 --> 00:04:50.334
"You made us root for
the Steelers, you bitch."

00:04:50.334 --> 00:04:51.934
[ Laughter ]

00:04:51.934 --> 00:04:54.300
So, anyway, but the reason
your name came up

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:57.367
is one of our writers walked
out, and he was wearing a mask,

00:04:57.367 --> 00:04:59.868
and Lizzo -- and again,
she'd had a few drinks.

00:04:59.868 --> 00:05:04.067
She thought it was Paul Rudd.
She thought it was you.

00:05:04.067 --> 00:05:05.334
So the writer's here today,

00:05:05.334 --> 00:05:07.534
but first I want to show
the first half of the clip,

00:05:07.534 --> 00:05:09.868
and then we're gonna bring out
the writer, Seth Reiss.

00:05:09.868 --> 00:05:13.400
So here's Lizzo noticing
our writer the first time.

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:14.868
-Ohh, I'm calm now.

00:05:14.868 --> 00:05:16.434
-Alright, let's bring him over.

00:05:16.434 --> 00:05:18.501
-Paul [bleep] Rudd!

00:05:18.501 --> 00:05:19.801
Paul Rudd!

00:05:19.801 --> 00:05:21.601
-Hey, Lizzo,
I know the real reason

00:05:21.601 --> 00:05:22.868
you're breaking up with me

00:05:22.868 --> 00:05:25.267
is that you met famous
Hollywood actor Paul Rudd,

00:05:25.267 --> 00:05:27.033
who's now gonna
take his mask off,

00:05:27.033 --> 00:05:29.000
and I want you to react to it
in real time.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:30.300
-[Bleep]

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:33.033
-Alright, so now we're gonna
bring out the writer.

00:05:33.033 --> 00:05:37.100
And we're gonna let you guys
decide, was Lizzo crazy?

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:40.834
[ Laughter, cheering ]

00:05:40.834 --> 00:05:43.667
Alright, you know what?

00:05:43.667 --> 00:05:46.901
Not -- Not --

00:05:46.901 --> 00:05:49.133
Not as bad as I thought.

00:05:49.133 --> 00:05:51.167
I owe an apology to Lizzo.

00:05:51.167 --> 00:05:54.133
But now this is
the really exciting part, guys.

00:05:54.133 --> 00:05:56.834
'Cause again, I think
you're agreeing with Lizzo.

00:05:56.834 --> 00:05:58.634
This is not too far afield,

00:05:58.634 --> 00:06:00.834
until you see the rest
of the face.

00:06:00.834 --> 00:06:02.567
[ Laughter ]

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:08.701
A lot of other stuff can happen.

00:06:10.267 --> 00:06:12.467
-This better not be --
This better --

00:06:12.467 --> 00:06:13.868
Oh [bleep]

00:06:13.868 --> 00:06:15.968
[ Laughter ]

00:06:18.501 --> 00:06:20.000
-I didn't even look.

00:06:22.334 --> 00:06:26.400
-I need a tissue.
I need a tissue, please.

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:28.801
No, here's the thing.

00:06:28.801 --> 00:06:32.701
You don't look anything
like [bleep] Paul Rudd.

00:06:32.701 --> 00:06:33.767
[ Laughter ]

00:06:33.767 --> 00:06:35.734
-There we go.
Let's see both faces.

00:06:35.734 --> 00:06:37.234
There we go.

00:06:37.234 --> 00:06:40.734
Paul and Seth Reiss,
brothers from another mother.

00:06:40.734 --> 00:06:42.567
-Thank you so much.
-It's like looking in a mirror.

00:06:42.634 --> 00:06:44.601
-Thank you so much.
-It's like looking in a mirror!

00:06:44.601 --> 00:06:47.968
-Thank you so much.
-And look at these two.

00:06:47.968 --> 00:06:49.400
Two snowflakes can be alike.

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:51.634
We'll be right back with more
from Paul Rudd, everybody.

